2. Names read consistently

In this print C, O, 0 and D, and B, E, 2 and 3, and U, J and L are often indistinguishable. Names are
read the same way wherever they occur, from how they are declared and used:

  ITC -> ITO in the main program: the time-step counter prints as ITO in the main program; C, O and 0 are hard to tell apart
  REAC -> READ in the main program: READ statements print as REAC: the right side of D is worn away
  PF -> PP in COMMON statements: COMMON /VAR/ declares PP (P prints like F); SOLVEQ has its own scalar PF
  CU -> DU everywhere: COMMON /PCCR/ declares DU, used in TRANF and SOLVEQ; D prints like C
  W0 -> WO everywhere: the previous-time-level array is WO, like UO and VO
  DEN0 -> DENO everywhere: the previous-time-level density is DENO, like UO and TMO
  UT -> U1 in WALLFN: WALLFN arguments and locals as printed: U1
  VT -> V1 in WALLFN: WALLFN arguments and locals as printed: V1
  WT -> W1 in WALLFN: WALLFN arguments and locals as printed: W1
  UC -> UO in WALLFN: WALLFN arguments and locals as printed: UO
  UZ -> U2 in WALLFN: WALLFN arguments and locals as printed: U2
  VZ -> V2 in WALLFN: WALLFN arguments and locals as printed: V2
  WZ -> W2 in WALLFN: WALLFN arguments and locals as printed: W2
  TALN1 -> TAUN1 in WALLFN: WALLFN arguments and locals as printed: TAUN1
  DOU -> DDU in WALLFN: WALLFN arguments and locals as printed: DDU
  TALN -> TAUN everywhere: wall shear stress TAUN (Appendix B); worn U prints like L
  DK0 -> DKO everywhere: previous-time-level arrays end in letter O (UO VO WO DKO DEO DENO TMO); D prints like 0
  DE0 -> DEO everywhere: previous-time-level arrays end in letter O (UO VO WO DKO DEO DENO TMO); D prints like 0
  TM0 -> TMO everywhere: previous-time-level arrays end in letter O (UO VO WO DKO DEO DENO TMO); D prints like 0
  U0 -> UO everywhere: previous-time-level arrays end in letter O (UO VO WO DKO DEO DENO TMO); D prints like 0
  V0 -> VO everywhere: previous-time-level arrays end in letter O (UO VO WO DKO DEO DENO TMO); D prints like 0
  0K -> DK everywhere: previous-time-level arrays end in letter O (UO VO WO DKO DEO DENO TMO); D prints like 0
  0E -> DE everywhere: previous-time-level arrays end in letter O (UO VO WO DKO DEO DENO TMO); D prints like 0
  JEC -> JBC in WALVAL: boundary index array JBC; B prints like E
  I2C -> IBC in BOUNC: boundary index array IBC; B prints like 2
  J2C -> JBC in BOUNC: boundary index array JBC; B prints like 2
  CE -> DE in NEWVIS: epsilon field DE, clamped at SMNUM
  YC -> MC in SOLVEQ: blockage flag MC as printed
  B3 -> BB in LINERX: local array BB from the DIMENSION statement
  B3 -> BB in DIRCOS: side BB in the law of cosines with AA and CC
  LCXI -> UCXI in SOLVEQ: UCXI=UE-UW is defined just above; worn U prints like L (LCXI would be an uninitialised integer)
  NC -> NO in DIRCOS: DO 10 K=1,NO like the I and J loops
  BS -> BB in DIRCOS: BB is the side length computed above
  LC -> LO in INIT: DO 10 I=1,LO like the J and K loops
  SY8 -> SYB in TRANF: bottom face, with SXB and SZB
  CDST -> DDST in SOLVEQ: DDST is the name assigned
  DOST -> DDST in SOLVEQ: DDST is the name assigned
  COST -> DDST in SOLVEQ: DDST is the name assigned
  CDNT -> DDNT in SOLVEQ: DDNT is the name used
  DONT -> DDNT in SOLVEQ: DDNT is the name used; D prints like O
  ANAE -> ANAB in SOLVEQ: ANAB, the sum of the link coefficients (Appendix B)
  PF -> PE in SOLVEQ: east-face pressure PE, with PW, in PCXI=PE-PW
  TJC1 -> TJO1 in WALLFN: dummy argument TJO1
  HINLM -> HINUM everywhere: HINUM, the large-number sentinel (worn U prints like L)
  ISWL -> ISWU in the main program: ISWU in COMMON /LIMT/; worn U prints like L
  SIGL -> SIGU in the main program: SIGU, passed to SOLVEQ for U, V and W; worn U prints like L
  TJ01 -> TJO1 in WALLFN: dummy argument TJO1, as used in the body (TJO is the Jacobian, Appendix B); O and 0 print alike
  L0 -> LO everywhere: Appendix B spells LO, MO, NO and FO with the letter O, like UO and DEO; the line printer prints O and 0 alike
  M0 -> MO everywhere: Appendix B spells LO, MO, NO and FO with the letter O, like UO and DEO; the line printer prints O and 0 alike
  N0 -> NO everywhere: Appendix B spells LO, MO, NO and FO with the letter O, like UO and DEO; the line printer prints O and 0 alike
  F0 -> FO everywhere: Appendix B spells LO, MO, NO and FO with the letter O, like UO and DEO; the line printer prints O and 0 alike
  DONW -> DDNW in SOLVEQ: DDNW, the north-west cross-diffusion coefficient, like DDNE, DDSE and DDSW; a worn D prints like O (DDET reads ODET on the next card)

3. Verification

FORTRAN VII D R05-01.00 on the emulated OS/32 compiles cns3d.f as follows (listing summary lines):

  00-CNS3D   CNS3D        NO  ERROR(S)    TABLE SPACE USED:    11 K
  01-DIRCOS  DIRCOS       NO  ERROR(S)    TABLE SPACE USED:     6 K
  02-TRANF   TRANF        NO  ERROR(S)    TABLE SPACE USED:    10 K
  03-INIT    INIT         NO  ERROR(S)    TABLE SPACE USED:     3 K
  04-NEWVIS  NEWVIS       NO  ERROR(S)    TABLE SPACE USED:     5 K
  05-SOLVEQ  SOLVEQ       NO  ERROR(S)    TABLE SPACE USED:    18 K
  06-LINERX  LINERX       NO  ERROR(S)    TABLE SPACE USED:     3 K
  07-BOUNC   BOUNC        NO  ERROR(S)    TABLE SPACE USED:     7 K
  08-WALLFN  WALLFN       NO  ERROR(S)    TABLE SPACE USED:     5 K
  09-SYMOUT  SYMOUT       NO  ERROR(S)    TABLE SPACE USED:     7 K
  10-WALVAL  WALVAL       NO  ERROR(S)    TABLE SPACE USED:     3 K

The table space of every routine equals the one printed in the listing.

Each statement's location code, the byte offset of its object code, was compared with the code printed
on the same card of the scan where the scan has one: 1381 of 1543 agree exactly. Of the other 162,
105 differ in one hex digit and 37 in two, which are misread digits of the scan, and 20 are
illegible on the scan. 80 of them are single statements between agreeing ones. In the short runs of
disagreeing statements the difference is a misread leading digit (an offset such as 8000 hex that returns to
zero after the run), not a change of size; no statement has the wrong size.

Location codes and table space do not see a misreading that keeps the size of the code: one variable for another
of the same type, a digit in a constant, + for -. Section 1 lists the ones found by checking names and by
running the program (TESTS).

4. gfortran

Not needed: gfortran accepts cns3d.f as it is.

  gfortran -std=legacy -fno-automatic -o cns3d cns3d.f

5. cns3d_fixed.f

cns3d.f with the problems in TESTS (notes 1 to 6) corrected, made with

  python3 test/case.py cns3d.f cns3d_fixed.f wallvis bounc unsteady outletsum tmsource tmcross wallk

  SOLVEQ, DO 22         F1 = VISE/SIGF on all nodes, not only interior ones (note 1)
  BOUNC                 each wall passes its own link to WALLFN: AS, AE, AW, AT, AB instead of AN
                        for the south, east, west, top and bottom walls (note 2)
  SOLVEQ                APO(I,J,K) left out of ANAB, so the unsteady term is counted once
                        (note 4)
  BC, EAST OUT          the outlet flow summed over J=2..JT+1, K=2..KT+1, the cells of the inlet sum
                        (note 3)

  SOLVEQ, TM-SOURCE     the empty branch completed (note 5): SU = APO*FO*TJO and SP = -APO*TJO, the
                        time term only. The link coefficients are those of the momentum equations
                        (Prandtl number SIGU = 1), as the listing has them; SP leaves out the mass
                        imbalance -CPO of the momentum cell, which for a scalar removes a uniform field
  BC, EAST OUT          TM carried to the outlet like U, V, W, K and E

  SOLVEQ, TM-SOURCE     the cross-derivative diffusion of a nonorthogonal grid added to SU, with the
                        coefficients DDNE ... DDSB of the other equations (option tmcross)
  WALLFN, K             near a wall only the production is replaced by the wall-function value; the
                        listing set SU = (CPO+APO)*K + production, which drops the cross-derivative
                        terms and takes the time term at the new K instead of the old, so the time
                        derivative cancels in wall cells (option wallk)

The temperature edits are new code: the listing has no temperature source to read. The temperature equation still
uses ISWW, ALW and ERRW of the W equation, so the W correction printed is that of TM when INSOT is set.
43 cards differ from cns3d.f. Like cns3d.f,
it is set up for case D: the outlet is corrected on V; the other cases take the same case.py options
as before, without wallvis and bounc.
